    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d10">

                <pb facs="0035" n="713" sameAs="frus:frus1863p2/pg713"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr.
                        Dayton</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 304.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Washington</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1863-02-20"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >February</hi> 20, 1863.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> You will probably have noticed that a bill
                    has passed the Senate conferring upon the President the power to issue letters
                    of marque and reprisal in any war in which this country may at any time be
                    engaged. It is expected that the bill will become a law. It is not unlikely, in
                    that event, that the measure may possibly be misapprehended abroad. Should this
                    prove to be the case at Paris, and explanations in regard to it should be asked
                    of you, or in your judgment be likely to prove useful, you may say that, as the
                    bill stands, the executive government will be left at liberty to put the law in
                    force in its discretion, and that thus far the proper policy in regard to the
                    exercise of that discretion has not engaged the President’s attention. If no
                    extreme circumstances shall exist when it may become expedient to put the act in
                    force against the insurgents, every proper effort will be made to prevent
                    surprise on the part of friendly nations, whose commerce and navigation it might
                    be feared would be incidentally and indirectly affected.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">William</hi> L. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Dayton</hi>, Esq., <hi rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c.,
                            &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
